location_notes = {
 'jabalpur':'For a business site in Jabalpur, I would separate the home page from detailed service explanations. The home page can orient a new visitor; a service page can answer scope, process and typical questions in enough depth to stand alone. A genuine project example is useful when it shows what was shipped and links to a live result.',
 'ranjhi':'A tutor could explain subjects, levels and enquiry timing, while a shop may need categories and practical visiting information. These are different jobs and should not be forced into the same template. A short contact form can carry the subject or product into a WhatsApp message so the first reply has context.',
 'adhartal':'For a repair or retail business, a visitor may be comparing options under time pressure. A service grid should use plain labels and avoid hiding essential details behind effects. If stock or availability changes daily, the site can present categories and invite a specific enquiry rather than displaying stale promises.',
 'vijay-nagar':'A launch page works best when it states one real offer and shows proof close to the claim it supports. If the product is new, explain the workflow and limitations honestly. We can watch where visitors stop reading, then refine the order of sections based on actual enquiry patterns rather than adding sections to look larger.',
 'napier-town':'A professional practice should make it easy to find the right service without turning every subject into a sales pitch. Credentials, approach and contact expectations can reduce uncertainty. If the work has legal, medical or financial implications, claims and testimonials need particular care and should be approved by the practice before publishing.',
 'wright-town':'An education or specialist site often serves more than one reader: a student, a parent, a patient or an administrator. Give each a clear route through the information. Dates, fees and eligibility must be owned by someone who can update them; a polished layout cannot compensate for out-of-date practical details.',
 'sadar':'For a shop or hospitality venue, the most useful first screen may be an offer summary with real hours and a clear route to call or visit. Social media can show updates, but a website can organize stable information in one reliable place. If menus change, an editable section is more useful than an old PDF.',
 'gwarighat':'An experience-led page should balance atmosphere with decision details. Visitors may want duration, inclusions, accessibility considerations and whether advance contact is necessary. Real images can give a feel for the experience, while structured copy helps people compare options. It is better to say what is known than to make broad travel claims.',
 'gorakhpur-jabalpur':'A focused service page can answer what is included, who it is for, what information is needed and what the next conversation looks like. The contact form can then ask only for the fields necessary to reply. Related project links should demonstrate actual capability, while guide links can answer a buyer’s deeper questions.',
 'katanga':'A creator’s site should give the work room to breathe without making every project look identical. One case study can show the challenge, selected design decisions and final link. A potential client also needs practical information: what kind of work is accepted, how an enquiry starts and where collaboration happens.',
 'madan-mahal':'For education providers, a visitor might need course details, schedule and admissions; for a small business, they may need service categories and contact. The navigation should reflect that distinction. If the site will grow, a simple content structure can keep new pages related to existing topics rather than creating a maze.',
 'garha':'A practical service site can use a short checklist of covered tasks and an honest service area. If work depends on inspection or availability, the page should explain that before inviting a request. Real photos and examples may help; invented testimonials or instant-service claims can mislead both visitors and the team answering enquiries.',
 'panagar':'Area coverage should be specific enough to help a visitor decide whether to contact you. If travel, remote delivery or delivery radius changes by service, describe those differences. A regional page should link directly to the actual service details and contact route, then be updated when coverage changes.',
 'sihora':'Performance begins with priorities: useful text and navigation should appear before large decorative assets. A responsive page can still use color, motion and imagery carefully. I would test the service and contact path on smaller screens, especially where forms, menus and long headings are most likely to cause friction.',
 'patan':'For a first website, collecting content is often the largest practical step. Write down the services, real operating details and common questions before choosing a visual style. Then publish a compact site with a dependable contact route. Add guides or case studies only when there is original information to share.',
 'bhedaghat':'Tourism pages should be clear about who runs an activity and how availability is confirmed. A gallery alone cannot answer what is included or where the meeting point is. A visitor should be able to send a date-specific enquiry, and the owner should have an easy way to revise seasonal information.',
 'bargi':'If several experiences or packages are offered, each can explain its duration, inclusions and booking step. Visitors planning on a phone should not need to decipher an image poster for key information. A small, editable set of pages can stay accurate as timings or offerings change.',
 'narsinghpur':'A regional agricultural catalog may benefit from separating fertilizers, seeds and crop-care categories so visitors can ask about the right product group. The site can present the shop’s actual strengths, location and contact without claiming live inventory. Product advice should remain with the business team.',
 'katni':'A business operating across cities may need one clear service structure and an honest coverage page. A visitor in Katni should still see the same real team, examples and contact details, not a fictional branch. Remote collaboration can be explained through a short process: discovery call, content review, build and launch checks.',
 'damoh':'An organization that publishes updates needs to decide who owns the information. A news area can become stale quickly if that responsibility is unclear. Start with stable pages describing the mission or service and add a publishing workflow only when staff can maintain it. New functionality should solve a proven visitor task.',
}
